{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"VirtualItem"},{"type":"text","value":" object represents a single item returned by the virtualizer. It contains information you need to render the item in the coordinate space within your virtualizer's scrollElement and other helpful properties/functions."}]},{"type":"code","value":"export interface VirtualItem {\n  key: string | number | bigint\n  index: number\n  start: number\n  end: number\n  size: number\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The following properties and methods are available on each VirtualItem object:"}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"key","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"key"}]},{"type":"code","value":"key: string | number | bigint","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The unique key for the item. By default this is the item index, but should be configured via the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"getItemKey"},{"type":"text","value":" Virtualizer option."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"index","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"index"}]},{"type":"code","value":"index: number","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The index of the item."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"start","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"start"}]},{"type":"code","value":"start: number","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The starting pixel offset for the item. This is usually mapped to a css property or transform like "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"top/left"},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"translateX/translateY"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"end","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"end"}]},{"type":"code","value":"end: number","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The ending pixel offset for the item. This value is not necessary for most layouts, but can be helpful so we've provided it anyway."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"size","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"size"}]},{"type":"code","value":"size: number","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The size of the item. This is usually mapped to a css property like "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"width/height"},{"type":"text","value":". Before an item is measured with the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"VirtualItem.measureElement"},{"type":"text","value":" method, this will be the estimated size returned from your "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"estimateSize"},{"type":"text","value":" virtualizer option. After an item is measured (if you choose to measure it at all), this value will be the number returned by your "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"measureElement"},{"type":"text","value":" virtualizer option (which by default is configured to measure elements with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"getBoundingClientRect()"},{"type":"text","value":")."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"lane","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"lane"}]},{"type":"code","value":"lane: number","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The lane index of the item. Items are assigned to the shortest lane. Lane assignments are cached immediately based on the size estimated by "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"estimateSize"},{"type":"text","value":" by default; set "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"laneAssignmentMode: 'measured'"},{"type":"text","value":" to base assignments on measured sizes instead.\nIn regular lists it will always be set to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"0"},{"type":"text","value":" but becomes useful for masonry layouts (see variable examples for more details)."}]}],"headings":[{"id":"key","text":"key","level":3},{"id":"index","text":"index","level":3},{"id":"start","text":"start","level":3},{"id":"end","text":"end","level":3},{"id":"size","text":"size","level":3},{"id":"lane","text":"lane","level":3}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\nThe `VirtualItem` object represents a single item returned by the virtualizer. It contains information you need to render the item in the coordinate space within your virtualizer's scrollElement and other helpful properties/functions.\n\n```tsx\nexport interface VirtualItem {\n  key: string | number | bigint\n  index: number\n  start: number\n  end: number\n  size: number\n}\n```\n\nThe following properties and methods are available on each VirtualItem object:\n\n### `key`\n\n```tsx\nkey: string | number | bigint\n```\n\nThe unique key for the item. By default this is the item index, but should be configured via the `getItemKey` Virtualizer option.\n\n### `index`\n\n```tsx\nindex: number\n```\n\nThe index of the item.\n\n### `start`\n\n```tsx\nstart: number\n```\n\nThe starting pixel offset for the item. This is usually mapped to a css property or transform like `top/left` or `translateX/translateY`.\n\n### `end`\n\n```tsx\nend: number\n```\n\nThe ending pixel offset for the item. This value is not necessary for most layouts, but can be helpful so we've provided it anyway.\n\n### `size`\n\n```tsx\nsize: number\n```\n\nThe size of the item. This is usually mapped to a css property like `width/height`. Before an item is measured with the `VirtualItem.measureElement` method, this will be the estimated size returned from your `estimateSize` virtualizer option. After an item is measured (if you choose to measure it at all), this value will be the number returned by your `measureElement` virtualizer option (which by default is configured to measure elements with `getBoundingClientRect()`).\n\n### `lane`\n\n```tsx\nlane: number\n```\n\nThe lane index of the item. Items are assigned to the shortest lane. Lane assignments are cached immediately based on the size estimated by `estimateSize` by default; set `laneAssignmentMode: 'measured'` to base assignments on measured sizes instead.\nIn regular lists it will always be set to `0` but becomes useful for masonry layouts (see variable examples for more details).","originalRawMarkdown":"---\ntitle: VirtualItem\n---\n\nThe `VirtualItem` object represents a single item returned by the virtualizer. It contains information you need to render the item in the coordinate space within your virtualizer's scrollElement and other helpful properties/functions.\n\n```tsx\nexport interface VirtualItem {\n  key: string | number | bigint\n  index: number\n  start: number\n  end: number\n  size: number\n}\n```\n\nThe following properties and methods are available on each VirtualItem object:\n\n### `key`\n\n```tsx\nkey: string | number | bigint\n```\n\nThe unique key for the item. By default this is the item index, but should be configured via the `getItemKey` Virtualizer option.\n\n### `index`\n\n```tsx\nindex: number\n```\n\nThe index of the item.\n\n### `start`\n\n```tsx\nstart: number\n```\n\nThe starting pixel offset for the item. This is usually mapped to a css property or transform like `top/left` or `translateX/translateY`.\n\n### `end`\n\n```tsx\nend: number\n```\n\nThe ending pixel offset for the item. This value is not necessary for most layouts, but can be helpful so we've provided it anyway.\n\n### `size`\n\n```tsx\nsize: number\n```\n\nThe size of the item. This is usually mapped to a css property like `width/height`. Before an item is measured with the `VirtualItem.measureElement` method, this will be the estimated size returned from your `estimateSize` virtualizer option. After an item is measured (if you choose to measure it at all), this value will be the number returned by your `measureElement` virtualizer option (which by default is configured to measure elements with `getBoundingClientRect()`).\n\n### `lane`\n\n```tsx\nlane: number\n```\n\nThe lane index of the item. Items are assigned to the shortest lane. Lane assignments are cached immediately based on the size estimated by `estimateSize` by default; set `laneAssignmentMode: 'measured'` to base assignments on measured sizes instead.\nIn regular lists it will always be set to `0` but becomes useful for masonry layouts (see variable examples for more details)."}
